"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[2182],{92182:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"slug":"uyazvimost-php2shell-sayty-bankov-oktyabr-2026","locale":"en","title":"PHP vulnerabilities prompt FSB instructions to disconnect bank and government sites that cannot update in time","description":"Two critical PHP vulnerabilities were found. The FSB instructed banks and government bodies to update or disconnect sites. Why one shared flaw can affect the whole internet.","blocks":[{"id":"b001","kind":"paragraph","text":"On October 21, PHP developers released fixes for two vulnerabilities collectively called PHP2Shell. Both received a severity score of 9.2 out of 10. They allow an attacker to execute code on a server and access data with a single unauthenticated request. Details were intended for later disclosure but leaked early. An Amazon honeypot detected the first attack attempt on October 17, 4 days before the fix. Wiz researchers believe the flaws are decades old, with modern artificial-intelligence models helping turn them into working exploits."},{"id":"b002","kind":"paragraph","text":"On October 22, the FSB\'s National Coordination Center for Computer Incidents sent a letter through GosSOPKA, the state computer-attack detection system. It went to critical-information-infrastructure organizations: government, finance, energy, transport and healthcare bodies. They were instructed to update PHP and scan servers with antivirus software. If rapid updating is impossible, external access to vulnerable servers must be restricted or the servers disconnected."},{"id":"b003","kind":"paragraph","text":"Imagine a town where almost every apartment entrance uses the same manufacturer\'s lock. One day, a single key is found to open them all, and copies are already circulating. Everyone must replace locks simultaneously, and until a locksmith arrives, keeping the entrance locked from inside is safer. Economists call this systemic risk: when everyone uses the same solution, its failure affects everyone at once. PHP is free and convenient, which explains its widespread use across the internet and why one flaw becomes a shared problem."},{"id":"b004","kind":"paragraph","text":"Sites have already been disconnected elsewhere. Britain temporarily closed 32 government services, calling it a precaution during updates. Government sites in Belgium, the Netherlands, Finland, France and Germany were also unavailable. Cybersecurity companies report millions of attempts to exploit the vulnerability. There are no reports yet of mass disconnections or breaches in Russia."},{"id":"b005","kind":"paragraph","text":"Users may find a bank website, government service or personal account temporarily inaccessible. That alone is not evidence of hacking: the organization may be blocking access while applying a fix. For a company, downtime costs lost sales and dissatisfied customers. A breach costs more: leaked data, fines, stolen funds and system recovery. That is why the FSB center favors a short outage."},{"id":"b006","kind":"paragraph","text":"Update time depends on the organization. Major banks with established processes can apply fixes within hours. Smaller companies and sites running unsupported older PHP versions may remain vulnerable for much longer. The scale of successful breaches will become clearer after companies inspect logs. Short website interruptions in the coming days usually indicate patching. An unpatched server continuing as though nothing happened is more dangerous."},{"id":"b007","kind":"heading","text":"What is the PHP2Shell vulnerability?"},{"id":"b008","kind":"paragraph","text":"Two flaws in the PHP programming language, CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619, scored 9.2 out of 10 for severity. An attacker can execute code on a website server and access data with one unauthenticated request. The fix was released on October 21, 2026."},{"id":"b009","kind":"heading","text":"Will bank and government websites stop working?"},{"id":"b010","kind":"paragraph","text":"Possibly during updates. On October 22, the FSB center instructed critical-infrastructure organizations, including banks and government bodies, to update PHP urgently. If that cannot be done quickly, access must be restricted or sites disconnected. No mass disconnections have yet been reported in Russia."},{"id":"b011","kind":"heading","text":"Why did one flaw affect so many sites?"},{"id":"b012","kind":"paragraph","text":"About 70% of websites worldwide and more than 90% of Russian-language sites use PHP. When millions of sites share one foundation, a flaw can expose all of them, and each owner must apply the fix."}],"sources":[{"label":"RBC: FSB recommends disconnecting websites because of PHP vulnerabilities","url":"https://www.kommersant.ru/doc/9045269"},{"label":"FSB center instructs organizations to disconnect sites over PHP flaws","url":"https://frankmedia.ru/311764"},{"label":"PHP2Shell. Critical PHP vulnerabilities patched, update now","url":"https://www.helpnetsecurity.com/2026/10/21/php2shell-php-vulnerabilities-patched-cve-2026-109618-cve-2026-109619/"},{"label":"PHP2Shell. Mass Exploitation Looms for Leaked Critical PHP Flaws","url":"https://www.securityweek.com/php2shell-mass-exploitation-looms-for-leaked-critical-php-flaws/"},{"label":"Websites across world hit by millions of attempts to exploit PHP code flaw","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/oct/22/websites-across-world-hit-by-millions-of-attempts-to-exploit-php-code-flaw"},{"label":"PHP2Shell puts dozens of UK government services out of service","url":"https://www.theregister.com/security/2026/10/22/php2shell-puts-dozens-of-uk-government-services-out-of-service/5304840"},{"label":"Two AI-discovered web-security vulnerabilities cause global alarm","url":"https://www.forbes.ru/tekhnologii/570823-vyavlennye-s-pomos-u-ii-dve-uazvimosti-v-veb-bezopasnosti-vyzvali-trevogu-v-mire"}],"sourceBodySha256":"e3fb2a77720fe8860c329873f043e4f7761ab7ab4c70849c9068a88ee60dd68e"}')}}]);